text:
Most template languages ask you to learn a second syntax and sprinkle it through your HTML. Chameleon asks you to learn a handful of attributes. A template is always a well-formed document, and every loop and condition attaches to the element it controls. The result is markup you can read top to bottom and know exactly which element repeats, which disappears, and where the data lands. The full language reference lives at chameleon.readthedocs.io; this page covers the parts the podcast site needs.

TAL cheat sheet
&lt;!-- Variable substitution: any Python expression inside ${ } --&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;${title}&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;${user.name.title()} has ${len(items)} item(s)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;!-- Conditionals: the element is omitted entirely when the expression is falsy --&gt;
&lt;div tal:condition="show_banner"&gt;Special offer!&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div tal:condition="not items"&gt;Nothing here yet.&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;!-- Loops --&gt;
&lt;li tal:repeat="item items"&gt;${item.name} - $${item.price}&lt;/li&gt;

&lt;!-- The repeat variable exposes index, number, even, odd, first and last --&gt;
&lt;tr tal:repeat="row data" class="${'odd' if repeat.row.odd else 'even'}"&gt;
    &lt;td&gt;${repeat.row.number}. ${row.title}&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;

&lt;!-- Attributes: interpolate directly, or set several with tal:attributes --&gt;
&lt;a href="/items/${item.id}" class="${'active' if is_current else ''}"&gt;${item.name}&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;a tal:attributes="href item.url; class item.css_class"&gt;${item.name}&lt;/a&gt;

&lt;!-- Replace element content, or the whole element --&gt;
&lt;span tal:content="message"&gt;placeholder shown in a browser preview&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span tal:replace="formatted_date"&gt;2024-01-01&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;!-- Define a local variable --&gt;
&lt;span tal:define="total sum(i.price for i in items)"&gt;Total: ${total}&lt;/span&gt;
Two habits pay off quickly. First, the placeholder text inside tal:content and tal:replace is what you see when you open the file in a browser without rendering it, so write something realistic there. Second, a literal $ followed by { is written $${...}, as in the price example above.


Escaping and structure
Everything inserted with ${...}, tal:content or tal:replace is HTML-escaped. A guest named &lt;script&gt; becomes harmless text. When you genuinely have markup that is already safe, say so with the structure keyword:
&lt;div tal:replace="structure rich_html_content" /&gt;
Treat that keyword as a promise. Only make it for HTML you generated or sanitized yourself.


Shared layouts with METAL
The podcast site has one navigation bar, one footer and one stylesheet link, and nobody wants to paste them into every page. METAL macros are Chameleon’s template inheritance. The layout defines slots; each page fills them.
templates/shared/layout.pt:
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;&lt;title&gt;${title}&lt;/title&gt;&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;main metal:define-slot="content"&gt;Default content&lt;/main&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
templates/home/index.pt:
&lt;metal:block use-macro="load: ../shared/layout.pt"&gt;
&lt;div metal:fill-slot="content"&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;${title}&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/metal:block&gt;
The load: path is resolved relative to the template that uses it, so a page in home/ reaches the layout with ../shared/layout.pt. Chameleon also falls back to the template folder, so shared/layout.pt works too. The example apps use the relative form.
The layout sees the same variables as the page, which is why title above comes straight from the handler’s dict. Set it once per handler and every page has a title.


File extensions
The loader does not care about the extension. .pt is the Chameleon convention, and auto-naming looks for .html first and .pt second (see the decorator guide). Any other extension works with an explicit path. The podcast’s episodes/feed.xml is a template like any other, served with content_type='application/xml'.


Alpine.js, htmx and other attribute syntaxes
Here is a surprise waiting for anyone who adds a sprinkle of Alpine.js to a Chameleon page. Chameleon runs with a restricted namespace by default, and attributes such as @click, :class, or x-on:click look to it like XML namespace prefixes it has never heard of. The template refuses to parse and you get a KeyError naming the attribute. The fix is one flag at startup:
chameleon_robyn.global_init(template_folder, restricted_namespace=False)
The same flag exists on ChameleonTemplate. With the restriction off, any attribute syntax passes through to the browser untouched.


Reusable components with chameleon-partials
The episode card appears on the episode list and again on the search results page. Copying it once is fine. Copying it a third time is how layouts drift. chameleon-partials adds render_partial() for rendering a sub-template from inside a template. It keeps its own loader, so initialize both packages at startup with the same folder:
import chameleon_partials
import chameleon_robyn

chameleon_robyn.global_init(template_folder, auto_reload=dev_mode)
chameleon_partials.register_extensions(template_folder, auto_reload=dev_mode)
Pass render_partial through the handler’s dict:
@app.get('/')
@chameleon_robyn.template('home/index.pt')
def index(request):
    return {
        'render_partial': chameleon_partials.render_partial,
        'episodes': get_episodes(),
    }
Then insert the partial with tal:replace="structure ...":
&lt;div tal:repeat="ep episodes"&gt;
    &lt;div tal:replace="structure render_partial('shared/episode_card.pt', ep=ep)" /&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
structure stops Chameleon from escaping the partial’s HTML, and tal:replace swaps out the placeholder element. The example-partials/ app in the repository is the core example refactored this way. Open its episode list template next to the one in example/ and watch a block of card markup collapse into one line.

Sooner or later someone will visit /episodes/9999. Maybe a link rotted, maybe they are poking at the URL to see what happens. What they should get is a page in your site’s own clothes that says the episode is not here and points the way home. What they should never get is a stack trace. One call makes that the default:
@app.get('/episodes/:episode_id')
@chameleon_robyn.template('episodes/detail.pt')
async def episode_detail(request, episode_id: int):
    episode = await get_episode(episode_id)
    if not episode:
        chameleon_robyn.not_found()  # Renders errors/404.pt with status 404

    return {'episode': episode}
Notice there is no return in front of not_found(). It never returns. It raises ChameleonRobynNotFoundException, the decorator catches it, renders the 404 template with status 404 and a text/html content type, and sends that Response instead. The handler’s own status_code and content_type settings do not apply to the 404. The rest of your handler can stay blissfully unaware that the lookup can fail.

The default template
By default the library renders errors/404.pt, relative to your template folder. Create that file in every app that calls not_found(). If it is missing, Chameleon raises a ValueError naming the file, which turns a friendly 404 into an unfriendly 500, so make it the second template you write.
The template receives one variable, message, with a short description of the 404. Render it or ignore it. Using the site layout keeps the lost visitor inside your navigation:
&lt;metal:block use-macro="load: ../shared/layout.pt"&gt;
&lt;div metal:fill-slot="content"&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Page not found&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;${message}&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;a href="/"&gt;Back to the home page&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/metal:block&gt;


A different 404 template
Perhaps the guest directory deserves its own “no such guest” page:
chameleon_robyn.not_found(four04template_file='errors/custom_404.pt')


Your own message
not_found() always sends the same generic message. To put your own words on the page, raise the exception directly. The decorator treats it exactly the same way:
from chameleon_robyn import ChameleonRobynNotFoundException

if not episode:
    raise ChameleonRobynNotFoundException(f'There is no episode {episode_id}.')
The second argument is the template path, defaulting to errors/404.pt.


Outside a decorated handler
not_found() only works inside handlers wrapped with @template, because the decorator is what catches the exception. Anywhere else, build the response yourself with response():
return chameleon_robyn.response('errors/404.pt', status_code=404, message='No such page.')


Other error pages
For a 500 page or any other status, response() is the tool, and it works inside decorated handlers too, because a returned Response passes through untouched:
@app.get('/report')
@chameleon_robyn.template('reports/view.pt')
def report(request):
    try:
        data = build_report()
    except ReportError as e:
        return chameleon_robyn.response('errors/500.pt', status_code=500, error=str(e))
    return {'data': data}

Quick start
Three files, three minutes. Here is the shortest path from an empty folder to a rendered page.
1. Set up your project structure:
my_app/
├── app.py
└── templates/
    └── home/
        └── index.pt
2. Create a template (templates/home/index.pt). It is plain HTML with two new ideas: ${name} drops in a value, and tal:repeat on an element makes that element repeat.
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Hello, ${name}!&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;ul&gt;
        &lt;li tal:repeat="item items"&gt;${item}&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
3. Wire it up (app.py). Three lines matter: the import, the global_init() call that says where templates live, and the decorator above the handler.
from pathlib import Path
from robyn import Robyn
import chameleon_robyn

app = Robyn(__file__)

# Point Chameleon at your templates folder (do this once at startup)
template_folder = (Path(__file__).resolve().parent / 'templates').as_posix()
chameleon_robyn.global_init(template_folder, auto_reload=True)

@app.get('/')
@chameleon_robyn.template('home/index.pt')
async def index(request):
    return {'name': 'World', 'items': ['Robyn', 'Chameleon', 'Python']}

app.start(host='127.0.0.1', port=8000)
That’s it. Your handler returns a dict, the @template decorator renders it through the Chameleon template and wraps the result in a Robyn Response. The handler never touched a string of HTML, and it never will. The getting started guide walks through this in more detail.

The @template decorator
A route handler has two jobs: decide what to show, and show it. @template takes the second job away entirely. It intercepts your handler’s return value and renders it through a Chameleon template, so the handler reads like a description of the page rather than a construction of it.

Explicit template path
@app.get('/episodes')
@chameleon_robyn.template('episodes/list.pt')
def episode_list(request):
    return {'episodes': get_all_episodes()}


Auto-naming (convention over configuration)
Leave the path out and the template is named after the code that renders it. A function called index in a module called home_views looks for home_views/index.html first and, if that file doesn’t exist, uses home_views/index.pt. Your template folder ends up mirroring your view modules, which is a pleasant thing to discover six months later.
# Looks for templates/home_views/index.pt
@app.get('/')
@chameleon_robyn.template()
def index(request):
    return {'message': 'Hello!'}
You can even skip the parentheses:
@app.get('/')
@chameleon_robyn.template
def index(request):
    return {'message': 'Hello!'}
Two details: a script run directly (python app.py) has the module name __main__, so the script’s file name is used instead and index() in app.py maps to app/index.pt (an existing __main__/index.pt is still found for apps built on the old behavior). And the name is resolved at the first request, not when the decorator runs, so global_init() can be called after the routes are decorated.


Async handlers - just works
The decorator notices async def and awaits the handler. Nothing to configure.
@app.get('/dashboard')
@chameleon_robyn.template('dashboard.pt')
async def dashboard(request):
    stats = await fetch_stats_from_db()
    return {'stats': stats}


Custom status codes and content types
Templates are not only for HTML pages. An XML feed is a template too, and so is the “created” page after a form post.
# Return a 201 after creating a resource
@app.post('/items')
@chameleon_robyn.template('items/created.pt', status_code=201)
async def create_item(request):
    item = await save_item(request.json())
    return {'item': item}

# Serve an XML feed
@app.get('/feed.xml')
@chameleon_robyn.template('feed.xml', content_type='application/xml')
def xml_feed(request):
    return {'episodes': get_recent_episodes()}


Response pass-through (redirects, errors, etc.)
Sometimes the right answer is not a page at all. If your handler returns a Robyn Response object directly, the decorator steps aside and passes it through untouched. This is how you handle redirects, custom error responses, or anything that shouldn’t be rendered through a template:
from robyn import Response, Headers

@app.get('/old-page')
@chameleon_robyn.template('home/index.pt')
def old_page(request):
    # This Response is returned as-is - no template rendering
    return Response(
        status_code=302,
        description='',
        headers=Headers({'Location': '/new-page'}),
    )


Modifying the response after rendering (__response_callback__)
Here is the classic puzzle. A visitor logs in. You want to render the welcome page, which means returning a dict, and you also want to set a session cookie, which means touching the Response. You cannot do both from a dict. Or can you?
Put a callable in your model under the reserved __response_callback__ key and the decorator invokes it with the freshly built Response after rendering:
from robyn import Response

@app.post('/account/login')
@chameleon_robyn.template('account/welcome.pt')
async def login(request):
    user = await authenticate(request)
    if not user:
        return chameleon_robyn.response('account/login_failed.pt', status_code=401)

    token = create_session_token(user)

    def set_session_cookie(resp: Response):
        resp.headers.append('Set-Cookie', f'session={token}; HttpOnly; Path=/')

    return {
        'user': user,
        '__response_callback__': set_session_cookie,
    }
A few details:

The key is popped before rendering, so it never reaches your template (and your dict isn’t mutated - returning a shared model is fine).
Mutate the Response in place; the callback’s return value is ignored.
The value must be callable - anything else raises ChameleonRobynException.

Everything the decorator does, including the exact return-value contract, is in the decorator guide.

Friendly 404 pages
Sooner or later someone will visit /episodes/9999. What they should get is a page in your site’s own clothes that points the way home, not a stack trace. Call not_found() from any decorated handler:
@app.get('/episodes/:episode_id')
@chameleon_robyn.template('episodes/detail.pt')
async def episode_detail(request, episode_id: int):
    episode = await get_episode(episode_id)
    if not episode:
        chameleon_robyn.not_found()  # Renders errors/404.pt with status 404

    return {'episode': episode}
Notice there is no return in front of not_found(). It never returns; the decorator catches what it raises and sends the 404 page instead. By default it renders errors/404.pt, but you can specify any template:
chameleon_robyn.not_found(four04template_file='errors/custom_404.pt')
The 404 template receives a message variable describing the 404, which you can render with ${message} if you want. To supply your own message, raise ChameleonRobynNotFoundException('your text') directly; the decorator handles it the same way. not_found() only works inside @template-decorated handlers; see friendly 404 pages for the alternatives.

ChameleonTemplate class (Robyn’s TemplateInterface)
If you prefer Robyn’s built-in template pattern, ChameleonTemplate implements the same render_template() interface as Robyn’s JinjaTemplate (via a structural protocol, so importing chameleon_robyn never pulls in Jinja2):
from chameleon_robyn import ChameleonTemplate

chameleon = ChameleonTemplate('templates/', auto_reload=True)

@app.get('/page')
def page(request):
    return chameleon.render_template('page.pt', title='Hello')
This is a standalone class with its own template loader - it doesn’t require global_init(). render_template() always returns a 200 text/html response.

Works with chameleon-partials
The episode card appears on the list page and again in search results. Copying it once is fine; copying it a third time is how layouts drift. chameleon-partials provides render_partial() for rendering sub-templates (think: reusable components). It works alongside chameleon-robyn with no extra configuration - just initialize both at startup:
import chameleon_partials
import chameleon_robyn

chameleon_robyn.global_init(template_folder, auto_reload=dev_mode)
chameleon_partials.register_extensions(template_folder, auto_reload=dev_mode)
Then pass render_partial through your template context:
import chameleon_partials

@app.get('/')
@chameleon_robyn.template('home/index.pt')
def index(request):
    return {
        'render_partial': chameleon_partials.render_partial,
        'episodes': get_episodes(),
    }
And use it in your templates with tal:replace="structure ...":
&lt;div tal:repeat="ep episodes"&gt;
    &lt;div tal:replace="structure render_partial('shared/episode_card.pt', ep=ep)" /&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
Note: use tal:replace="structure ..." (not ${structure ...}) when rendering partials. The structure keyword tells Chameleon to insert the HTML without escaping, and tal:replace swaps out the placeholder element entirely.

Template language cheat sheet
Chameleon uses TAL (Template Attribute Language). Most template languages ask you to learn a second syntax; Chameleon asks you to learn a handful of attributes. Here are the ones you will reach for every day:
&lt;!-- Variable substitution --&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;${title}&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;${user.name}&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;!-- HTML-safe output (skip auto-escaping) --&gt;
&lt;div tal:replace="structure rich_html_content" /&gt;

&lt;!-- Conditionals --&gt;
&lt;div tal:condition="show_banner"&gt;Special offer!&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div tal:condition="not items"&gt;Nothing here yet.&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;!-- Loops --&gt;
&lt;li tal:repeat="item items"&gt;${item.name} - $${item.price}&lt;/li&gt;

&lt;!-- Repeat with index --&gt;
&lt;tr tal:repeat="row data" class="${'odd' if repeat.row.odd else 'even'}"&gt;
    &lt;td&gt;${repeat.row.number}. ${row.title}&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;

&lt;!-- Attributes --&gt;
&lt;a href="/items/${item.id}" class="${'active' if is_current else ''}"&gt;
    ${item.name}
&lt;/a&gt;

&lt;!-- Layout inheritance with METAL macros --&gt;
&lt;!-- layout.pt --&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;main metal:define-slot="content"&gt;Default content&lt;/main&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;

&lt;!-- page.pt --&gt;
&lt;metal:block use-macro="load: shared/layout.pt"&gt;
&lt;div metal:fill-slot="content"&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;My page content here&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/metal:block&gt;
Full docs: chameleon.readthedocs.io. The writing templates guide covers layouts, escaping, and partials in more depth.

A route handler has two jobs: decide what to show, and show it. @template takes the second job away entirely. Your handler gathers data and returns a dict. The decorator finds the template, renders it, builds the Response, and sets the headers. You never see a string of HTML in Python again, and the handler reads like a description of the page rather than a construction of it.

The contract
Everything the decorator does follows from one rule about what your handler returns:







Handler returns
What the decorator does




a dict
Renders the template with the dict as its variables and returns a 200 Response (or the status you configured).


a Robyn Response
Returns it untouched. No template is rendered. This is the escape hatch for redirects and hand-built responses.


anything else
Raises ChameleonRobynException at request time. None, strings and numbers are all rejected.



Return the dict directly. The decorator copies it before rendering, so returning a shared or module-level dict is safe.


Explicit template path
The podcast’s episode list is the plainest possible case:
@app.get('/episodes')
@chameleon_robyn.template('episodes/list.pt')
def episode_list(request):
    return {'episodes': get_all_episodes()}
The path is relative to the folder passed to global_init(). Any file extension works; .pt is the convention, and the same site renders a .xml feed with the same decorator.


Auto-naming
Leave the path out and the decorator names the template after the code that renders it. The rule has three steps:

Take the last segment of the handler’s module name and the function name.
Look for &lt;module&gt;/&lt;function&gt;.html under the template folder.
If that file does not exist, use &lt;module&gt;/&lt;function&gt;.pt.

So index() in views/home_views.py renders home_views/index.html if it exists, otherwise home_views/index.pt. Your template folder ends up mirroring your view modules, which is a pleasant thing to discover six months later.
# Looks for templates/home_views/index.pt
@app.get('/')
@chameleon_robyn.template()
def index(request):
    return {'message': 'Hello!'}
The parentheses are optional:
@app.get('/')
@chameleon_robyn.template
def index(request):
    return {'message': 'Hello!'}
Two details are worth knowing:

A script run directly (python app.py) has the module name __main__, which would make for a strange folder name. The library uses the script’s file name instead, so index() in app.py maps to app/index.pt. If no such file exists but __main__/index.html or __main__/index.pt does, that is used, so apps built on the old behavior keep working.
The name is resolved at the first request, not when the decorator runs. That is what lets you decorate routes at import time and call global_init() later from main(). The file only has to exist by the time the first visitor arrives.



Async handlers
Nothing to configure. The decorator notices async def and awaits the handler, so the episode page can go to the database like any other coroutine:
@app.get('/dashboard')
@chameleon_robyn.template('dashboard.pt')
async def dashboard(request):
    stats = await fetch_stats_from_db()
    return {'stats': stats}
Robyn’s typed path parameters pass straight through:
@app.get('/episodes/:episode_id')
@chameleon_robyn.template('episodes/detail.pt')
async def episode_detail(request, episode_id: int):
    return {'episode': await get_episode(episode_id)}


Status codes and content types
Templates are not only for HTML pages. The podcast feed is a Chameleon template too, and so is a “created” page after a form post:
# Return a 201 after creating a resource
@app.post('/items')
@chameleon_robyn.template('items/created.pt', status_code=201)
async def create_item(request):
    item = await save_item(request.json())
    return {'item': item}

# Serve an XML feed
@app.get('/feed.xml')
@chameleon_robyn.template('episodes/feed.xml', content_type='application/xml')
def xml_feed(request):
    return {'episodes': get_recent_episodes()}
The charset=utf-8 suffix is added to the content type for you. These settings apply to rendered responses only. A pass-through Response keeps its own status and headers, and a 404 from not_found() always renders as text/html with status 404.


Redirects and other pass-through responses
Sometimes the right answer is not a page at all. Return a Robyn Response and the decorator steps aside:
from robyn import Response, Headers

@app.get('/old-page')
@chameleon_robyn.template('home/index.pt')
def old_page(request):
    return Response(
        status_code=302,
        description='',
        headers=Headers({'Location': '/new-page'}),
    )
The same rule lets one handler choose between templates at runtime: build the alternative with response() and return it.


Setting cookies or headers on a rendered page
Here is the classic puzzle. A visitor logs in. You want to render the welcome page, which means returning a dict, and you also want to set a session cookie, which means touching the Response. You cannot do both from a dict. Or can you?
Put a callable under the reserved key __response_callback__ in the returned dict. After rendering, the decorator calls it with the Response it just built:
from robyn import Response

@app.post('/account/login')
@chameleon_robyn.template('account/welcome.pt')
async def login(request):
    user = await authenticate(request)
    if not user:
        return chameleon_robyn.response('account/login_failed.pt', status_code=401)

    token = create_session_token(user)

    def set_session_cookie(resp: Response):
        resp.headers.append('Set-Cookie', f'session={token}; HttpOnly; Path=/')

    return {
        'user': user,
        '__response_callback__': set_session_cookie,
    }
Rules for the callback:

It is removed from the model before rendering, so the template never sees it, and your dict is not modified.
Change the Response in place. The return value is ignored.
It must be callable. Anything else raises ChameleonRobynException.



Decorator order
Robyn’s route decorator goes on the outside and @template goes directly above the function. Robyn reads the handler’s signature when the route is registered, and the decorator preserves the original signature, so request and path parameters keep flowing exactly as they did before.
@app.get('/episodes')                          # outermost
@chameleon_robyn.template('episodes/list.pt')  # directly above the function
def episode_list(request):
    ...
